# Argus configuration — copy to .env or export directly
# Auth — first source wins:
#   1) ANTHROPIC_API_KEY (or --api-key)        -> x-api-key billing
#   2) ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN / CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN (or --auth-token)
#                                              -> Bearer, Claude Pro/Max subscription
#   3) auto: ~/.claude/.credentials.json       -> Claude Code sign-in, auto-refreshed
#   4) auto: opencode auth.json [anthropic]    -> OpenCode sign-in
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=sk-ant-oat01-...
# Optional overrides:
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://your-gateway/v1   (real env only - NOT loaded
#   from .env: a .env inside an untrusted dir must not redirect the API
#   endpoint and exfiltrate the key)
# ARGUS_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5                (claude-sonnet-4-5 was
#   the example here and retires 2026-11-30 - `argus models` lists what
#   this build prices, and an unpriced model reports no cost rather than a
#   wrong one)
# ARGUS_MCP_CONFIG=/path/to/mcp.json           (real env only - NOT loaded
#   from .env: it names subprocesses to run)
# ARGUS_SECRETS=pw1;token2                     (; separated - redacted from every artifact)
# ARGUS_SEAL_KEY=/path/to/keyfile              (real env only - NOT loaded
#   from .env, and that is the point: a .env inside an untrusted directory
#   must not be able to choose the HMAC key for the transcript chain, or
#   planted config could produce a chain that verifies. A path is read as a
#   key file so the key stays out of the process listing. Unkeyed, the chain
#   proves the file was not edited after the run; keyed, only a holder of
#   the key can produce one that verifies - `argus verify --seal-key ...`)
# ARGUS_MCP_CWD=1                              (real env only - also search
#   ./mcp.json for MCP servers. Off by default for the same reason as
#   ARGUS_MCP_CONFIG: the file names subprocesses to run)
# ARGUS_EXPLOITS_CWD=1                         (real env only - also load
#   ./resources/exploits/. Off by default: a manifest names a command, so a
#   planted one in an untrusted working directory would run through the
#   model)
